      ---------------------------------------------------------------
              Fuzzing harness for the carrier extraction paths
      ---------------------------------------------------------------


1. Theory
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The YAML configuration is written by the operator, but the propagation headers
are not: traceparent, tracestate and baggage arrive from whoever sent the
request, so in a deployment behind HAProxy they are the library's one genuinely
remote input.  Every byte of them flows through the SDK propagators and then
through the wrapper's carrier iteration, text map construction and span context
handling, all of which allocate on paths a malformed header can steer.  The unit
tests cover the header shapes a reviewer thinks of; a fuzzer covers the ones
nobody thought of.

Coverage-guided fuzzing only works when the fuzzer can observe the code that it
exercises.  The harness therefore does not link against the regular build of the
library: fuzz-build.sh builds a separate copy of it with clang, compiled with
the -fsanitize=address,fuzzer-no-link options, so every branch taken inside the
library feeds the mutation engine and the address sanitizer checks every heap
access and reports leaked allocations on every error path.  Because autotools
refuses an out-of-tree build while the source tree itself is configured, that
copy is a snapshot of the tree, configured and built inside the fuzzing build
directory: a harness builds whether or not the tree is configured, and the tree
is never written to.  The snapshot leaves the tree's build artifacts behind, so
no object built by another compiler can survive into the instrumented library,
and it is rebuilt from scratch as soon as a library source is newer than that
library, so a campaign never runs against stale code.

The harness (fuzz-extract.cpp) creates one tracer during initialization and
reuses it for every input, which keeps the iteration rate high and puts the
whole budget into the extraction code rather than into provider setup.  Each
input is cut into lines and each line is split at the first colon into a header
name and its value, so the mutation engine can steer the name and the value
independently; a line without a colon becomes a traceparent value, which keeps
the W3C parser reachable even from a corpus of bare header values.  The result
is presented twice, once through a text map reader and once through an HTTP
headers reader, since the two carriers reach the propagators through different
wrapper templates.  Whatever span context comes back is destroyed again, and the
first input line is additionally passed to otelc_span_context_create() as a
trace state header, with the identifier bytes taken from the input as well, so
the tracestate parser and the identifier validation both run.  Everything the
harness allocates per input is released before the next one, so the leak checker
sees the carrier and span context lifecycle; the single context and tracer live
for the whole process and their teardown paths are the one part this harness
does not exercise.

2. Practice
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Usage, with the time limit in seconds and any further arguments passed on to
the libFuzzer binary unchanged:

  % ./fuzz-extract.sh [ time-limit [ libfuzzer-args ... ] ]

The corpus persists under /tmp/otelc-fuzz/corpus-extract and is seeded with a
W3C traceparent and tracestate pair, a baggage header with a percent-encoded
value, a B3 header and a bare traceparent value; the crash artifacts are written
to the /tmp/otelc-fuzz directory.  The script requires clang, because libFuzzer
and the coverage instrumentation are clang features, and it reads its
configuration file through the OTELC_FUZZ_CFG environment variable, pointed at
test/otel-cfg.yml.  The speed_test context is used deliberately: its exporter
writes to /dev/null, so no campaign fills a log file.

The first campaign ran about three and a half million executions at roughly
28000 runs a second, growing the corpus to a bit over six hundred units, and
finished without any findings at all: no crashes, no address sanitizer reports,
no memory leaks, no timeouts, and no aborts escaping the propagators.

3. Conclusion
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The extraction paths withstood a coverage-guided, sanitizer-instrumented attack
on the library's remote input surface: malformed, truncated and hostile headers
are rejected without corrupting or leaking memory, and an invalid carrier never
produces a span context that the caller then has to distrust.

The result is a statement about the configured backend and the default
propagator set only.  Fuzzing the composite propagator build, running the same
harness under the thread sanitizer with several tracers, and feeding it a
dictionary built from the header grammars are the natural extensions when more
assurance is wanted.
